IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lpatas-dates`

-- Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas), Part 3.3 (Costs Disclosure and Assessment) -- the provisions a client meets when
-- a law practice acts for them, for example on a no-win no-fee agreement for an injury or compensation claim, asked
-- from the seat of the client.
--
-- SEAT: the client of the law practice (the person for whom legal services are provided), and, where the Act gives
-- them the client's rights, an associated third party payer (a person who owes the law practice the client's costs).
-- The law practice, the costs assessor, the Supreme Court, the Board and the Law Society get no seat: what they must or
-- may do appears as what the client is owed, what may be done to the client, and by when.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. Two duties are regulative rules run by EVALTRACE inside a question: the law practice's duty to give an
-- itemised bill within 21 days (s 316(2), MUST) and the bar on suing for costs until 30 days after the bill (ss 313(1),
-- 316(4), (5), SHANT).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Division 1 (ss 283, 284); Division 2 (ss 285, 286, 287(1), (2)); Division 3 (ss 291(1), (4), 292,
-- 294(1), (2), 295(1), (2), (5), 296, 297, 300(1), (2), (3), (5), 301(1), (2)); Division 4 (ss 303, 305); Division 5
-- (ss 306(2) to (6), 307, 308, 309, 311, 312(1), (5), (6), (12)); Division 6 (ss 313(1) to (3), 314(1), 316(1), (2),
-- (4), (5)); Division 7 (ss 319(1), (2), (5), (6), 321(4), 323(b), 328(1), 331(2), (3), (6), 333(1), 334(1), (2), 338).
-- What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas), No. 24 of 2007, the consolidated version published 2026-05-05 and current
-- on 6 November 2026, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-2007-024.txt. The Legal Profession Regulations
-- (which may set a higher disclosure threshold, s 295(1)(a), the maximum interest rate, s 305(4), and excluded matters,
-- s 307(2)) are not deposited: those numbers are asked as facts.
--
-- Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (CC BY 4.0, State of Tasmania). For the
-- latest information on Tasmanian Government legislation please go to www.legislation.tas.gov.au.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to someone else are INPUTS. Answers follow
-- the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words,
-- NOTHING where it does not answer.

§ `Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas), Part 3.3: a client's questions about legal costs`

§§ `Division 1 -- who I am: third party payers and sophisticated clients`

-- s 284(1): "a person is a third party payer, in relation to a client of a law practice, if the person is not the
-- client and -- (i) is under a legal obligation to pay all or any part of the legal costs for legal services provided
-- to the client; or (ii) being under that obligation, has already paid all or a part of those legal costs; and (b) a
-- third party payer is an associated third party payer if the legal obligation ... is owed to the law practice, whether
-- or not it is also owed to the client or another person; and (c) ... a non-associated third party payer if the legal
-- obligation ... is owed to the client or another person but not the law practice." (3): "A law practice that retains
-- another law practice on behalf of a client is not on that account a third party payer".
DECLARE `My obligation to pay` IS ONE OF
    `I am the client myself`
    `I am legally obliged to pay some or all of the client's costs, and I owe that to the law practice`
    `I am legally obliged to pay some or all of the client's costs, but I owe that only to the client or someone else`
    `I am a law practice that retained the other law practice for the client, and owe nothing else`
    `I have no legal obligation to pay the client's costs`

DECLARE `Third party payer status` IS ONE OF
    `an associated third party payer (s 284(1)(b))`
    `a non-associated third party payer (s 284(1)(c))`
    `not a third party payer (s 284(1), (3))`

-- A "means"-style definition ("is ... if"): outside it, a plain no.
@export Am I a "third party payer" for someone else's legal costs, and if so of which kind (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 284)? A person other than the client who is legally obliged (by contract, legislation or otherwise) to pay some or all of the client's legal costs is a third party payer: "associated" if the obligation is owed to the law practice, "non-associated" if it is owed only to the client or someone else. An associated third party payer gets most of the client's rights about disclosure and costs.
GIVEN `my obligation` IS A `My obligation to pay` @desc Which describes you, in relation to the client's legal costs?
GIVETH A `Third party payer status`
`s 284 -- am I a third party payer` `my obligation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my obligation`
    WHEN `I am legally obliged to pay some or all of the client's costs, and I owe that to the law practice` THEN `an associated third party payer (s 284(1)(b))`
    WHEN `I am legally obliged to pay some or all of the client's costs, but I owe that only to the client or someone else` THEN `a non-associated third party payer (s 284(1)(c))`
    OTHERWISE `not a third party payer (s 284(1), (3))`

-- s 295(1)(c): the clients to whom disclosure need not be made; (d): costs agreed as a result of a tender process.
-- s 283: "sophisticated client means a client to whom, because of section 295(1)(c) or (d), disclosure under section
-- 291 or section 293(1) is not or was not required".
DECLARE `Who the client is` IS ONE OF
    `an individual, or another client not in the list below`
    `a law practice or an Australian legal practitioner (s 295(1)(c)(i))`
    `a public company, large proprietary company, foreign company, a subsidiary of one, or a registered Australian body (s 295(1)(c)(ii))`
    `a financial services licensee (s 295(1)(c)(iii))`
    `a liquidator, administrator or receiver (s 295(1)(c)(iv))`
    `a professional services partnership of more than 20 members, or that would be a large proprietary company (s 295(1)(c)(v))`
    `a joint-venture company or group meeting s 295(1)(c)(vi) or (vii)`
    `a Minister, government department or public authority (s 295(1)(c)(viii))`

GIVEN `who the client is` IS A `Who the client is`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 295(1)(c) -- the client is in the list` `who the client is` MEANS
    (NOT (`who the client is` EQUALS `an individual, or another client not in the list below`))

-- A "means" definition: outside (c) and (d), a plain no.
@export Am I a "sophisticated client" (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 283, 295(1)(c), (d))? A sophisticated client is one to whom costs disclosure need not be made because of who the client is (a law practice or lawyer, a public or large company, a financial services licensee, a liquidator, administrator or receiver, a large professional partnership, certain joint ventures, or government), or because the costs were agreed through a tender. Sophisticated clients lose several protections: the conditional-agreement formalities, the uplift disclosure, the rights statement with a bill, and the Supreme Court's power to extend the 60 days for assessment; and they may contract out of costs assessment.
GIVEN `who the client is` IS A `Who the client is` @desc Who is the client? An individual (a person bringing an injury claim, for example) is the first choice.
      `the costs were agreed through a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the legal costs, or the basis for calculating them, agreed as a result of a tender process?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 283 -- am I a sophisticated client` `who the client is` `the costs were agreed through a tender` MEANS
        `s 295(1)(c) -- the client is in the list` `who the client is`
    OR  `the costs were agreed through a tender`

§§ `Division 2 -- does this Part apply to my matter`

-- s 286(4): "A notification has no effect for the purposes of subsection (2)(b) if it is given after the period of 28
-- days after the law practice discloses to the client (under a corresponding law) information about the client's right
-- to make a notification of that kind".
@export By what day must I notify the law practice in writing that I want Tasmania's costs law (Part 3.3) to apply to my matter, if it told me of that right under another jurisdiction's law (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 286(4))? Within 28 days after it disclosed that right; a later notice has no effect (an agreement with the law practice can still be made at any time).
GIVEN `the day I was told of the right` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice disclosed to you, under the other jurisdiction's law, your right to notify that you want this Part to apply. ISO date.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 286(4) -- by what day must I notify` `the day I was told of the right` MEANS
    `days after` `the day I was told of the right` 28

-- s 285: "This Part applies to a matter if the client first instructs the law practice in relation to the matter in
-- this jurisdiction." s 286(1): it also applies if (a) it does not currently apply or the place of first instructions
-- cannot be determined, (b) the services are wholly or primarily in Tasmania or the matter has a substantial connection
-- with Tasmania, and (c) the client accepts a written offer that it apply or notifies in writing that it is required.
-- s 287(2): it ceases to apply if the services are wholly or primarily in another jurisdiction or the matter has a
-- substantial connection with it, and the client agrees or notifies under that jurisdiction's corresponding law. "if":
-- outside the conditions, a plain no. Fork F1: s 287(3) (a later agreement or notification bringing the Part back) is
-- not modelled; the s 290 split between periods is out.
@export Does Tasmania's law on costs disclosure, costs agreements and costs assessment (Part 3.3 of the Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas)) apply to my matter (ss 285 to 287)? It applies if you first instructed the law practice in Tasmania (in person, by post, phone, email or otherwise); or, failing that, if the work is wholly or mainly done in Tasmania or the matter is substantially connected with it and you accepted a written offer that it apply, or notified the law practice in writing (within 28 days after being told of the right) that you require it. It stops applying if the matter belongs to another jurisdiction and you agreed or notified under that jurisdiction's law that its law apply.
GIVEN `I first instructed the law practice in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the law practice first receive your instructions about this matter in Tasmania (in person, by post, phone, fax, email or otherwise)?
      `it cannot be determined where I first instructed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it impossible to tell in which jurisdiction you first instructed the law practice?
      `the work is wholly or mainly in Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the legal services being provided wholly or primarily in Tasmania?
      `the matter is substantially connected with Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter have a substantial connection with Tasmania (the regulations may set rules for this)?
      `I accepted a written offer that this Part apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you accept (in writing or by conduct) a written offer from the law practice to agree that Tasmania's Part 3.3 apply to the matter?
      `I notified in writing that I require this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you notify the law practice in writing that you require Tasmania's Part 3.3 to apply?
      `I was told of my right to notify` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the law practice disclose to you, under another jurisdiction's law, your right to give that notification?
      `the day I was told` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I notified` IS A DATE @desc If you notified, the day you did. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the matter belongs to another jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the services wholly or primarily provided in another jurisdiction, or is the matter substantially connected with another jurisdiction?
      `I chose the other jurisdiction's law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you agree with the law practice, or notify it in time, under the other jurisdiction's law, that its costs law apply to the matter?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`ss 285 to 287 -- does Part 3.3 apply to my matter` `I first instructed the law practice in Tasmania` `it cannot be determined where I first instructed` `the work is wholly or mainly in Tasmania` `the matter is substantially connected with Tasmania` `I accepted a written offer that this Part apply` `I notified in writing that I require this Part` `I was told of my right to notify` `the day I was told` `the day I notified` `the matter belongs to another jurisdiction` `I chose the other jurisdiction's law` MEANS
    `it applies` AND (NOT `it is displaced`)
    WHERE
        `the notice counts` MEANS
                `I notified in writing that I require this Part`
            AND (   (NOT `I was told of my right to notify`)
                 OR `the day I notified` `is on or before` (`s 286(4) -- by what day must I notify` `the day I was told`))
        `by s 286` MEANS
                ((NOT `I first instructed the law practice in Tasmania`) OR `it cannot be determined where I first instructed`)
            AND (`the work is wholly or mainly in Tasmania` OR `the matter is substantially connected with Tasmania`)
            AND (`I accepted a written offer that this Part apply` OR `the notice counts`)
        `it applies` MEANS `I first instructed the law practice in Tasmania` OR `by s 286`
        `it is displaced` MEANS `the matter belongs to another jurisdiction` AND `I chose the other jurisdiction's law`

§§ `Division 3 -- what I must be told about costs`

-- s 295(1): disclosure "is not required to be made in any of the following circumstances: (a) if the total legal costs
-- in the matter, excluding disbursements, are not likely to exceed $1 500 (exclusive of GST) or the prescribed amount
-- (whichever is higher); (b) if -- (i) the client has received one or more disclosures under section 291 or section
-- 293(1) from the law practice in the previous 12 months; and (ii) the client has agreed in writing to waive the right
-- to disclosure; and (iii) a principal of the law practice decides on reasonable grounds that ... the further
-- disclosure is not warranted; (c) [the listed clients]; (d) [tender]; (e) if the client will not be required to pay
-- the legal costs or they will not otherwise be recovered by the law practice; (f) in any circumstances prescribed by
-- the regulations." (2): if the law practice becomes aware that the costs are likely to exceed the amount, it must
-- disclose as soon as practicable.
`the disclosure threshold in the Act` MEANS 1500

GIVEN `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 295(1)(a) -- the threshold` `the prescribed amount` MEANS `the larger of` `the disclosure threshold in the Act` `the prescribed amount`

DECLARE `Whether I must be told` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the law practice must give me written costs disclosure (ss 291, 294)`
    `no: the likely costs are at most the threshold, but if they become likely to exceed it I must be told as soon as practicable (s 295(1)(a), (2))`
    `no: I waived it in writing after a disclosure in the last 12 months, and a principal decided further disclosure is not warranted (s 295(1)(b))`
    `no: I am a client in the s 295(1)(c) list (a sophisticated client)`
    `no: the costs were agreed through a tender (s 295(1)(d))`
    `no: I will not have to pay the costs (s 295(1)(e))`
    `no: the regulations exempt this case (s 295(1)(f))`

-- The exceptions in the Act's order; the first that applies is reported (fork F2). "is not required ... in any of the
-- following circumstances": a closed list, so outside it the duty stands.
@export Must the law practice give me written costs disclosure (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 291, 294, 295)? Yes, unless: the total legal costs (not counting disbursements) are not likely to exceed $1,500 excluding GST (or a higher amount the regulations prescribe); you had a disclosure from it in the last 12 months, waived further disclosure in writing, and a principal decided on reasonable grounds it is not warranted; you are a law practice, lawyer, large company, government or another listed client; the costs were set by tender; you will not have to pay (pro bono); or the regulations exempt the case.
GIVEN `the likely total costs` IS A NUMBER @desc Your best figure for the total legal costs in the matter, in dollars, excluding disbursements (outlays) and GST.
      `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the Legal Profession Regulations prescribe for s 295(1)(a), in dollars, if any. Enter 0 if you do not know; the Act's $1,500 then applies.
      `I had a disclosure from this law practice in the last 12 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you received one or more costs disclosures from this law practice in the previous 12 months?
      `I agreed in writing to waive disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you agree in writing to waive your right to costs disclosure?
      `a principal decided on reasonable grounds that it is not warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a principal of the law practice decide, on reasonable grounds, that further disclosure is not warranted?
      `who the client is` IS A `Who the client is` @desc Who is the client?
      `the costs were agreed through a tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the costs, or their basis, agreed as a result of a tender process?
      `I will not have to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it settled that you will not have to pay the legal costs and the law practice will not otherwise recover them (for example, it acts pro bono)? A no-win no-fee agreement is not this: answer no.
      `the regulations exempt this case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the Legal Profession Regulations exempt this case from disclosure? Answer no if you do not know.
GIVETH A `Whether I must be told`
`s 295 -- must the law practice give me costs disclosure` `the likely total costs` `the prescribed amount` `I had a disclosure from this law practice in the last 12 months` `I agreed in writing to waive disclosure` `a principal decided on reasonable grounds that it is not warranted` `who the client is` `the costs were agreed through a tender` `I will not have to pay` `the regulations exempt this case` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the likely total costs` AT MOST (`s 295(1)(a) -- the threshold` `the prescribed amount`)
            THEN `no: the likely costs are at most the threshold, but if they become likely to exceed it I must be told as soon as practicable (s 295(1)(a), (2))`
        IF `I had a disclosure from this law practice in the last 12 months` AND `I agreed in writing to waive disclosure` AND `a principal decided on reasonable grounds that it is not warranted`
            THEN `no: I waived it in writing after a disclosure in the last 12 months, and a principal decided further disclosure is not warranted (s 295(1)(b))`
        IF `s 295(1)(c) -- the client is in the list` `who the client is`
            THEN `no: I am a client in the s 295(1)(c) list (a sophisticated client)`
        IF `the costs were agreed through a tender`
            THEN `no: the costs were agreed through a tender (s 295(1)(d))`
        IF `I will not have to pay`
            THEN `no: I will not have to pay the costs (s 295(1)(e))`
        IF `the regulations exempt this case`
            THEN `no: the regulations exempt this case (s 295(1)(f))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the law practice must give me written costs disclosure (ss 291, 294)`

-- s 291(1): the law practice must disclose (a) the basis of calculation, including whether a scale applies; (b) the
-- client's right to (i) negotiate a costs agreement, (ii) receive a bill, (iii) request an itemised bill within 30 days
-- after receipt of a lump sum bill, (iv) be notified of any substantial change; (c) an estimate of the total legal
-- costs, or a range and an explanation of the major variables; (d) details of the billing intervals (if any); (e) the
-- rate of interest (if any) on overdue costs; (f) if the matter is litigious, an estimate of the range of costs
-- recoverable if successful and payable if unsuccessful; (g) the right to progress reports; (h) whom to contact; (i) the
-- avenues in a dispute (costs assessment, setting aside, a complaint under Chapter 4); (j) their time limits; (k) that
-- Tasmanian law applies; (l) the right to choose another jurisdiction's law. (4): (f) must include a statement that a
-- costs order will not necessarily cover all the client's costs, and (if applicable) that disbursements may be payable
-- even under a conditional costs agreement. s 292: the name of the practitioner who will primarily do the work, and
-- whether someone other than a legal practitioner will do any of it. (5): (b)(i) to (iii), (g), (i), (j) and (l) are
-- met by the prescribed form.
DECLARE `What is missing from my disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `nothing: everything ss 291 and 292 require was disclosed`
    `the basis on which costs are calculated, and whether a scale applies (s 291(1)(a))`
    `my rights to negotiate a costs agreement, get a bill, ask for an itemised bill within 30 days, and be told of changes (s 291(1)(b))`
    `an estimate of total costs, or a range and the major variables (s 291(1)(c))`
    `the billing intervals (s 291(1)(d))`
    `the interest rate on overdue costs (s 291(1)(e))`
    `in a litigious matter, the range of costs I may recover or be ordered to pay, with the s 291(4) statements (s 291(1)(f))`
    `my right to progress reports (s 291(1)(g))`
    `whom I can contact about costs (s 291(1)(h))`
    `the dispute avenues and their time limits (s 291(1)(i), (j))`
    `that Tasmanian law applies, and my right to choose another jurisdiction's law (s 291(1)(k), (l))`
    `the name of the lawyer who will mainly do the work, and whether non-lawyers will do some (s 292)`

-- The first missing item in the Act's order is reported (fork F2). (d) and (e) are needed only where the law practice
-- bills at intervals or charges interest ("(if any)"); (f) only in a litigious matter. s 283: "litigious matter means a
-- matter that involves, or is likely to involve, the issue of proceedings in a court or tribunal".
@export Did the law practice tell me everything about costs that it had to (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 291, 292)? Where disclosure is required, it must tell you in writing: how costs are calculated; your rights (to negotiate a costs agreement, get a bill, ask for an itemised bill, be told of changes, get progress reports); an estimate of the total costs; billing intervals and interest, if any; in a court or tribunal matter, the range of costs you may recover or have to pay; whom to contact; the dispute avenues and time limits; that Tasmanian law applies and your right to choose another jurisdiction's; and who will mainly do the work. A failure has consequences under s 300.
GIVEN `I was told how costs are calculated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told the basis on which legal costs will be calculated, including whether a scale of costs applies?
      `I was told my rights about agreements and bills` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told of your rights to negotiate a costs agreement, receive a bill, request an itemised bill within 30 days after a lump sum bill, and be notified of substantial changes (the prescribed form is enough)?
      `I was given an estimate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given an estimate of the total legal costs (or, if not practicable, a range and the major variables)?
      `I will be billed at intervals` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will you be billed at set intervals?
      `I was told the billing intervals` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, were you told the intervals? Otherwise answer no.
      `the law practice charges interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the law practice charge interest on overdue legal costs?
      `I was told the interest rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, were you told the rate (or the benchmark rate)? Otherwise answer no.
      `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter involve, or is it likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal?
      `I was told the litigation cost ranges` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, were you given an estimate of the range of costs you may recover if you win and may be ordered to pay if you lose, with a statement that a costs order will not necessarily cover all your costs (and, if applicable, that disbursements may be payable under a conditional agreement)? Otherwise answer no.
      `I was told of progress reports` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told of your right to progress reports?
      `I was told whom to contact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given details of the person to contact to discuss the costs?
      `I was told the dispute avenues and time limits` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told of costs assessment, setting aside a costs agreement and making a complaint, and their time limits?
      `I was told Tasmanian law applies and of my right to choose another` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told that Tasmanian law applies to the costs, and of your right to agree or notify that another jurisdiction's law apply?
      `I was told who will mainly do the work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you told in writing the name of the lawyer who will mainly do the work, and whether anyone who is not a lawyer will do any of it?
GIVETH A `What is missing from my disclosure`
`ss 291, 292 -- was I told everything` `I was told how costs are calculated` `I was told my rights about agreements and bills` `I was given an estimate` `I will be billed at intervals` `I was told the billing intervals` `the law practice charges interest` `I was told the interest rate` `the matter is litigious` `I was told the litigation cost ranges` `I was told of progress reports` `I was told whom to contact` `I was told the dispute avenues and time limits` `I was told Tasmanian law applies and of my right to choose another` `I was told who will mainly do the work` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I was told how costs are calculated`)
            THEN `the basis on which costs are calculated, and whether a scale applies (s 291(1)(a))`
        IF (NOT `I was told my rights about agreements and bills`)
            THEN `my rights to negotiate a costs agreement, get a bill, ask for an itemised bill within 30 days, and be told of changes (s 291(1)(b))`
        IF (NOT `I was given an estimate`)
            THEN `an estimate of total costs, or a range and the major variables (s 291(1)(c))`
        IF `I will be billed at intervals` AND (NOT `I was told the billing intervals`)
            THEN `the billing intervals (s 291(1)(d))`
        IF `the law practice charges interest` AND (NOT `I was told the interest rate`)
            THEN `the interest rate on overdue costs (s 291(1)(e))`
        IF `the matter is litigious` AND (NOT `I was told the litigation cost ranges`)
            THEN `in a litigious matter, the range of costs I may recover or be ordered to pay, with the s 291(4) statements (s 291(1)(f))`
        IF (NOT `I was told of progress reports`)
            THEN `my right to progress reports (s 291(1)(g))`
        IF (NOT `I was told whom to contact`)
            THEN `whom I can contact about costs (s 291(1)(h))`
        IF (NOT `I was told the dispute avenues and time limits`)
            THEN `the dispute avenues and their time limits (s 291(1)(i), (j))`
        IF (NOT `I was told Tasmanian law applies and of my right to choose another`)
            THEN `that Tasmanian law applies, and my right to choose another jurisdiction's law (s 291(1)(k), (l))`
        IF (NOT `I was told who will mainly do the work`)
            THEN `the name of the lawyer who will mainly do the work, and whether non-lawyers will do some (s 292)`
        OTHERWISE `nothing: everything ss 291 and 292 require was disclosed`

-- s 296(1): "If a law practice negotiates the settlement of a litigious matter on behalf of a client, the law practice
-- must disclose to the client, before the settlement is executed -- (a) a reasonable estimate of the amount of legal
-- costs payable by the client if the matter is settled (including any legal costs of another party that the client is
-- to pay); and (b) a reasonable estimate of any contributions towards those costs likely to be received from another
-- party." (2): a law practice retained through another need not, if the other makes the disclosure. "If": outside it, a
-- plain no.
@export Before I sign a settlement of my court or tribunal case, must my law practice tell me what costs I will have to pay (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 296)? Yes, if it negotiates the settlement of a litigious matter for you: before the settlement is executed it must give you a reasonable estimate of the legal costs you will pay if the matter settles (including any of the other side's costs you are to pay) and of any contribution to those costs you are likely to receive from another party. A law practice retained through another law practice need not, if the other one tells you.
GIVEN `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter involve, or is it likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal?
      `the law practice negotiates the settlement for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is this law practice negotiating the settlement on your behalf?
      `it was retained for me by another law practice that has told me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was this law practice (for example a barrister) retained for you by another law practice, and has that other law practice already given you the estimates before the settlement is executed?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 296 -- must I be told the costs before settling` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice negotiates the settlement for me` `it was retained for me by another law practice that has told me` MEANS
        `the matter is litigious`
    AND `the law practice negotiates the settlement for me`
    AND (NOT `it was retained for me by another law practice that has told me`)

-- s 297(1): "If a costs agreement involves an uplift fee, the law practice must, before entering into the agreement,
-- disclose to the client in writing -- (a) the law practice's legal costs; and (b) the uplift fee (or the basis of
-- calculation of the uplift fee); and (c) the reasons why the uplift fee is warranted." (2): not to a sophisticated
-- client. s 283: "uplift fee means additional legal costs (excluding disbursements) payable under a costs agreement on
-- the successful outcome of the matter to which the agreement relates."
@export Before I sign a costs agreement with an uplift fee (an extra fee if I win), must the law practice tell me in writing what it is and why (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 297)? Yes, unless you are a sophisticated client: before the agreement is made it must disclose in writing its legal costs, the uplift fee (or how it is calculated) and why the uplift fee is warranted.
GIVEN `the agreement has an uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the costs agreement provide for additional legal costs (other than disbursements) payable if the matter succeeds?
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a sophisticated client (a law practice, large company, government body or other client listed in s 295(1)(c), or were the costs agreed by tender)? An individual usually is not.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 297 -- must I be told about the uplift fee first` `the agreement has an uplift fee` `I am a sophisticated client` MEANS
    `the agreement has an uplift fee` AND (NOT `I am a sophisticated client`)

-- s 300(1): "If a law practice does not disclose to a client or an associated third party payer anything required by
-- this Division to be disclosed, the client or associated third party payer (as the case may be) need not pay the
-- legal costs unless they have been assessed under Division 7." (5): where the first law practice failed solely because
-- the retained law practice did not give it the information, (1) to (4) do not apply to the first law practice's own
-- costs, to that extent. (6): disclosure to one of client and payer does not help the other.
-- Answers: JUST TRUE while unassessed; JUST FALSE once assessed (the "unless" met: s 300 no longer excuses payment,
-- fork F3); NOTHING where s 300 does not apply (the Act does not answer here whether payment can be held back).
@export Because the law practice did not tell me something about costs it had to, can I hold off paying its legal costs until they have been assessed (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 300(1), (5))? Yes: a client (or associated third party payer) who was not told something Division 3 required need not pay the costs unless they have been assessed under Division 7. Once they have been assessed, this no longer excuses payment. It does not protect a law practice's own costs where its failure was caused solely by a law practice it retained not giving it the information.
GIVEN `something required was not disclosed to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the law practice fail to disclose to you something that Division 3 required (see the disclosure questions)?
      `the failure was caused solely by a law practice it retained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did this law practice fail only because another law practice it retained for you (a barrister, say) did not give it the information it needed, and are these this law practice's own costs?
      `the costs have been assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the legal costs been assessed by a costs assessor under Division 7?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 300(1) -- may I hold off paying until the costs are assessed` `something required was not disclosed to me` `the failure was caused solely by a law practice it retained` `the costs have been assessed` MEANS
    IF (NOT `something required was not disclosed to me`) OR `the failure was caused solely by a law practice it retained`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the costs have been assessed`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 301(1): a law practice must give a client, on reasonable request, (a) a written report of the progress of the
-- matter and (b) a written report of the legal costs incurred to date or since the last bill. (2): "A law practice may
-- charge a client a reasonable amount for a report under subsection (1)(a) but must not charge a client for a report
-- under subsection (1)(b)." s 302(4): an associated third party payer has the client's right to (b) reports.
DECLARE `The report I asked for` IS ONE OF
    `a written report on the progress of my matter (s 301(1)(a))`
    `a written report of the costs incurred to date or since the last bill (s 301(1)(b))`

DECLARE `What the report costs me` IS ONE OF
    `it must be given on reasonable request, and I may be charged a reasonable amount for it (s 301(2))`
    `it must be given on reasonable request, free: the law practice must not charge for it (s 301(2))`

@export Can the law practice charge me for a report on my matter or my costs (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 301)? On reasonable request it must give you a written progress report (for which it may charge a reasonable amount) and a written report of the costs incurred so far or since the last bill (which must be free).
GIVEN `the report I asked for` IS A `The report I asked for` @desc Which report did you ask for?
GIVETH A `What the report costs me`
`s 301 -- can I be charged for the report` `the report I asked for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the report I asked for`
    WHEN `a written report on the progress of my matter (s 301(1)(a))` THEN `it must be given on reasonable request, and I may be charged a reasonable amount for it (s 301(2))`
    WHEN `a written report of the costs incurred to date or since the last bill (s 301(1)(b))` THEN `it must be given on reasonable request, free: the law practice must not charge for it (s 301(2))`

§§ `Division 4 -- on what basis I pay, and interest`

-- s 303: "Subject to this Division, legal costs are recoverable -- (a) under a costs agreement made in accordance with
-- Division 5 or the corresponding provisions of a corresponding law; or (b) if paragraph (a) does not apply, in
-- accordance with an applicable scale of costs; or (c) if neither paragraph (a) nor (b) applies, according to the fair
-- and reasonable value of the legal services provided." s 311(2): costs under a void agreement are recoverable as set
-- out in s 303.
DECLARE `The basis of my costs` IS ONE OF
    `under my costs agreement (s 303(a))`
    `under the applicable scale of costs (s 303(b))`
    `the fair and reasonable value of the services (s 303(c))`

@export On what basis can the law practice recover its legal costs from me (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 303)? Under a costs agreement properly made under Division 5 (not void); if there is none, under an applicable scale of costs; if neither, at the fair and reasonable value of the legal services.
GIVEN `I have a valid costs agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you have a costs agreement made in accordance with Division 5 (or another jurisdiction's corresponding law) that is not void? See the s 311 question.
      `a scale of costs applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a scale of costs apply to the work?
GIVETH A `The basis of my costs`
`s 303 -- on what basis are my costs recoverable` `I have a valid costs agreement` `a scale of costs applies` MEANS
    IF `I have a valid costs agreement`
    THEN `under my costs agreement (s 303(a))`
    ELSE IF `a scale of costs applies`
         THEN `under the applicable scale of costs (s 303(b))`
         ELSE `the fair and reasonable value of the services (s 303(c))`

-- s 305(1): "A law practice may charge interest on unpaid legal costs if the costs are unpaid 30 days or more after the
-- practice has given a bill for the costs in accordance with this Part."
@export From what day can the law practice start charging interest on my unpaid bill (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 305(1))? From the 30th day after it gave you the bill, if the costs are still unpaid (a costs agreement may provide otherwise, s 305(2)).
GIVEN `the day the bill was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you the bill. ISO date.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 305(1) -- from what day can interest be charged` `the day the bill was given` MEANS
    `days after` `the day the bill was given` 30

-- s 305(2): interest may also be charged in accordance with a costs agreement. (3): "A law practice must not charge
-- interest under subsection (1) or (2) on unpaid legal costs unless the bill for those costs contains a statement that
-- interest is payable and of the rate of interest." (4): "A law practice may not charge interest under this section or
-- under a costs agreement at a rate that exceeds the rate prescribed by the regulations." "must not ... unless", "may
-- not": a plain no when a condition fails.
@export May the law practice charge me interest on my unpaid legal costs (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 305)? Only if the bill states that interest is payable and the rate; the rate is no more than the maximum the regulations prescribe; and either the costs have been unpaid for 30 days or more after the bill was given, or your costs agreement provides for interest.
GIVEN `the day the bill was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you the bill. ISO date.
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today). ISO date.
      `the bill states that interest is payable and the rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the bill contain a statement that interest is payable and of the rate?
      `my costs agreement provides for interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your costs agreement provide for interest on unpaid costs?
      `the rate charged` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate the law practice charges, in percent a year (for example 8 for 8%).
      `the prescribed maximum rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum rate prescribed by the Legal Profession Regulations, in percent a year.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 305 -- may the law practice charge me interest` `the day the bill was given` `the day asked about` `the bill states that interest is payable and the rate` `my costs agreement provides for interest` `the rate charged` `the prescribed maximum rate` MEANS
        `the bill states that interest is payable and the rate`
    AND `the rate charged` AT MOST `the prescribed maximum rate`
    AND (   (`s 305(1) -- from what day can interest be charged` `the day the bill was given`) `is on or before` `the day asked about`
         OR `my costs agreement provides for interest`)

§§ `Division 5 -- my costs agreement`

-- s 306(2): "A costs agreement must be written or evidenced in writing." (3): it "may consist of a written offer in
-- accordance with subsection (4) that is accepted in writing or by other conduct." (4): "The offer must clearly state --
-- (a) that it is an offer to enter a costs agreement; and (b) that the offer can be accepted in writing or by other
-- conduct; and (c) the type of conduct that will constitute acceptance." (5): "Except as provided by section 338
-- (Contracting out of Division by sophisticated clients), a costs agreement cannot provide that the legal costs to which
-- it relates are not subject to costs assessment under Division 7." Fork F5: (4) is applied to every agreement made by
-- accepting a written offer, whether accepted in writing or by conduct.
GIVEN `it is written or evidenced in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was made by my accepting a written offer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 306 -- the agreement is properly made` `it is written or evidenced in writing` `it was made by my accepting a written offer` `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires` `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment` `I am a sophisticated client` MEANS
        `it is written or evidenced in writing`
    AND ((NOT `it was made by my accepting a written offer`) OR `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires`)
    AND ((NOT `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment`) OR `I am a sophisticated client`)

-- s 307(2): "A conditional costs agreement may relate to any matter, except a matter that involves criminal
-- proceedings or proceedings under the Adoption Act 1988, Children, Young Persons and Their Families Act 1997, Youth
-- Justice Act 1997 or Relationships Act 2003 or under the Family Law Act 1975 of the Commonwealth or a matter prescribed
-- by the regulations." "except": in the list, a plain no.
DECLARE `The kind of matter` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings`
    `proceedings under the Adoption Act 1988`
    `proceedings under the Children, Young Persons and Their Families Act 1997`
    `proceedings under the Youth Justice Act 1997`
    `proceedings under the Relationships Act 2003`
    `proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cth)`
    `a matter the regulations exclude`
    `any other matter (for example an injury, compensation, insurance or employment claim)`

@export Can my matter be run on a conditional ("no-win no-fee") costs agreement (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 307(2))? Yes for any matter except criminal proceedings, proceedings under the Adoption Act 1988, the Children, Young Persons and Their Families Act 1997, the Youth Justice Act 1997, the Relationships Act 2003 or the Family Law Act 1975 (Cth), or a matter the regulations exclude. Injury and compensation claims are not excluded.
GIVEN `the kind of matter` IS A `The kind of matter` @desc What kind of matter is it?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 307(2) -- can my matter be no-win no-fee` `the kind of matter` MEANS
    `the kind of matter` EQUALS `any other matter (for example an injury, compensation, insurance or employment claim)`

-- s 307(3): "A conditional costs agreement -- (a) must set out the circumstances that constitute the successful outcome
-- of the matter to which it relates; and (b) may provide for disbursements to be paid irrespective of the outcome of
-- the matter; and (c) must be -- (i) in writing; and (ii) in clear plain language; and (iii) signed by the client; and
-- (d) must contain a statement that the client has been informed of the client's right to seek independent legal advice
-- before entering into the agreement; and (e) must contain a cooling-off period of not less than 5 clear business days
-- during which the client, by written notice, may terminate the agreement." (4), (5): (c)(iii), (d) and (e) do not
-- apply to an agreement between law practices (s 306(1)(c)) or with a sophisticated client.
`the shortest cooling-off period in business days` MEANS 5

GIVEN `it sets out what counts as success` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is in clear plain language` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I signed it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the cooling-off business days it gives` IS A NUMBER
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is between two law practices` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 307(3) -- the conditional agreement meets the requirements` `it sets out what counts as success` `it is in writing` `it is in clear plain language` `I signed it` `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `I am a sophisticated client` `it is between two law practices` MEANS
        `it sets out what counts as success`
    AND `it is in writing`
    AND `it is in clear plain language`
    AND (   `I am a sophisticated client`
         OR `it is between two law practices`
         OR (    `I signed it`
             AND `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice`
             AND `the cooling-off business days it gives` AT LEAST `the shortest cooling-off period in business days`))

@export Does my conditional ("no-win no-fee") costs agreement meet the Act's requirements (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 307(2), (3))? The matter must be one that may have a conditional agreement, and the agreement must set out what counts as a successful outcome, be in writing and in clear plain language, and (unless you are a sophisticated client) be signed by you, state that you were told of your right to get independent legal advice first, and give you a cooling-off period of at least 5 clear business days to end it by written notice. An agreement that fails is void (s 311).
GIVEN `the kind of matter` IS A `The kind of matter` @desc What kind of matter is it?
      `it sets out what counts as success` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement set out the circumstances that make up a successful outcome of the matter?
      `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the agreement in writing?
      `it is in clear plain language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it in clear plain language?
      `I signed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you sign it?
      `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it contain a statement that you were informed of your right to seek independent legal advice before entering into it?
      `the cooling-off business days it gives` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days of cooling-off (during which you may end it by written notice) does it give? 0 if none.
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a sophisticated client (see the s 283 question)? An individual usually is not.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 307 -- does my no-win no-fee agreement meet the requirements` `the kind of matter` `it sets out what counts as success` `it is in writing` `it is in clear plain language` `I signed it` `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `I am a sophisticated client` MEANS
        `s 307(2) -- can my matter be no-win no-fee` `the kind of matter`
    AND `s 307(3) -- the conditional agreement meets the requirements` `it sets out what counts as success` `it is in writing` `it is in clear plain language` `I signed it` `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `I am a sophisticated client` FALSE

-- s 307(3)(e): "a cooling-off period of not less than 5 clear business days". The period is counted from the day after
-- the agreement was made ("clear" days); fork F4 for statutory holidays.
@export By what day can I end my no-win no-fee agreement under its cooling-off period (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 307(3)(e))? The agreement must give at least 5 clear business days, counted from the day after it was made and skipping Saturdays, Sundays and Tasmanian statutory holidays; the answer is the last day of the period the agreement gives (or of 5 business days, if it gives fewer).
GIVEN `the day the agreement was made` IS A DATE @desc The day you entered into (signed) the agreement. ISO date.
      `the cooling-off business days it gives` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days of cooling-off does the agreement give? If it gives fewer than 5, enter the number anyway.
      `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Tasmanian statutory holidays (including a holiday observed on a Monday in place of one on a weekend) fall on a weekday (Monday to Friday) within the cooling-off period, counted to the end of the period as the holidays extend it? Usually 0.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 307(3)(e) -- by what day can I cool off` `the day the agreement was made` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period` MEANS
    `the last of the business days after` `the day the agreement was made` (`the larger of` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `the shortest cooling-off period in business days`) `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period`

-- s 307(3)(e): "during which the client, by written notice, may terminate the agreement." A period the text fixes:
-- outside it, a plain no. Fork F6: where the agreement gives fewer than 5 days (and is void for that), the 5 days are
-- used.
@export Did I end my no-win no-fee agreement in time, within its cooling-off period (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 307(3)(e), (6))? Yes, if you gave the law practice written notice ending it on or before the last day of the cooling-off period (at least 5 clear business days after the agreement was made, not counting weekends and statutory holidays).
GIVEN `the day the agreement was made` IS A DATE @desc The day you entered into (signed) the agreement. ISO date.
      `the cooling-off business days it gives` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days of cooling-off does the agreement give?
      `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc How many Tasmanian statutory holidays (including a holiday observed on a Monday in place of one on a weekend) fall on a weekday within the cooling-off period, counted to the end of the period as the holidays extend it? Usually 0.
      `I gave written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you give the law practice written notice ending the agreement?
      `the day of my notice` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you gave it. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 307(6) -- did I cool off in time` `the day the agreement was made` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period` `I gave written notice` `the day of my notice` MEANS
        `I gave written notice`
    AND `the day the agreement was made` `falls before or on the day of` `the day of my notice`
    AND `the day of my notice` `is on or before` (`s 307(3)(e) -- by what day can I cool off` `the day the agreement was made` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `statutory holidays on weekdays in the period`)

GIVEN `the first` IS A DATE, `the second` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first` `falls before or on the day of` `the second` IF `the first` `is on or before` `the second`

-- s 307(6): "If a client terminates an agreement within the period referred to in subsection (3)(e), the law practice
-- -- (a) may recover only those legal costs in respect of legal services performed for the client before that
-- termination that were performed on the instructions of the client and with the client's knowledge that the legal
-- services would be performed during that period; and (b) ... may not recover the uplift fee (if any)." "only": the
-- other work is not recoverable. Outside the period: NOTHING (s 307(6) does not apply; the agreement governs).
@export If I end my no-win no-fee agreement in the cooling-off period, what can the law practice still charge me (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 307(6))? Only the costs of work done before you ended it that was done on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done during the cooling-off period; never the uplift fee. If you did not end it in time, this rule does not apply (nothing).
GIVEN `I ended it in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you end the agreement by written notice within the cooling-off period (see the s 307(6) question)?
      `costs of work on my instructions knowing it would be done in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs, in dollars, of work done before you ended the agreement, on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done during the cooling-off period (not counting any uplift fee).
      `costs of other work before I ended it` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs, in dollars, of any other work done before you ended it (without your instructions or knowledge). It is not recoverable; it is asked so the answer can show that.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 307(6) -- what can I be charged after cooling off` `I ended it in time` `costs of work on my instructions knowing it would be done in the period` `costs of other work before I ended it` MEANS
    IF `I ended it in time`
    THEN JUST `costs of work on my instructions knowing it would be done in the period`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 308(4): "If a conditional costs agreement relates to a litigious matter -- (a) the agreement must not provide for
-- the payment of an uplift fee unless the law practice has a reasonable belief that a successful outcome of the matter
-- is reasonably likely; and (b) the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the legal costs (excluding disbursements)
-- otherwise payable." Outside a litigious matter the Act sets no cap: NOTHING.
`the uplift cap for a litigious matter` MEANS 25%

@export What is the largest uplift fee (extra fee if I win) my no-win no-fee agreement can charge (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 308(4)(b))? In a matter that involves or is likely to involve court or tribunal proceedings, 25% of the legal costs (excluding disbursements) otherwise payable. Outside litigation the Act sets no cap (nothing), though the fee must still be disclosed and explained.
GIVEN `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter involve, or is it likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal?
      `the legal costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs, in dollars, excluding disbursements and the uplift fee, payable under the agreement.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 308(4) -- the largest uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the legal costs otherwise payable` MEANS
    IF `the matter is litigious`
    THEN JUST (`the legal costs otherwise payable` TIMES `the uplift cap for a litigious matter`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 308(1): "A conditional costs agreement may provide for the payment of an uplift fee." (2): "The basis of
-- calculation of the uplift fee must be separately identified in the agreement." (3): "The agreement must contain an
-- estimate of the uplift fee or, if that is not reasonably practicable -- (a) a range of estimates ...; and (b) an
-- explanation of the major variables". (4) as above. (5): "A law practice must not enter into a costs agreement in
-- contravention of this section. Penalty: Fine not exceeding 100 penalty units." Only a conditional agreement may carry
-- an uplift fee (fork F7).
GIVEN `the agreement is conditional` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the uplift basis is separately identified` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the uplift fee` IS A NUMBER
      `the legal costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 308 -- the uplift fee is allowed` `the agreement is conditional` `the uplift basis is separately identified` `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` `the uplift fee` `the legal costs otherwise payable` MEANS
        `the agreement is conditional`
    AND `the uplift basis is separately identified`
    AND `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee`
    AND (   (NOT `the matter is litigious`)
         OR (    `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely`
             AND `the uplift fee` AT MOST (`the legal costs otherwise payable` TIMES `the uplift cap for a litigious matter`)))

@export Is the uplift fee (extra fee if I win) in my costs agreement allowed (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 308)? Only a conditional (no-win no-fee) agreement may have one; its basis of calculation must be separately identified; the agreement must estimate it (or give a range and the major variables); and in a court or tribunal matter the law practice must reasonably believe success is reasonably likely and the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the other legal costs (excluding disbursements). Breaking this is an offence (up to 100 penalty units), the agreement is void, and the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 311(4)).
GIVEN `the agreement is conditional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is payment of some or all of the legal costs conditional on a successful outcome (a no-win no-fee agreement)?
      `the uplift basis is separately identified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the basis on which the uplift fee is calculated separately identified in the agreement?
      `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement contain an estimate of the uplift fee (or, if not practicable, a range of estimates and an explanation of the major variables)?
      `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter involve, or is it likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal?
      `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, did the law practice have a reasonable belief that a successful outcome was reasonably likely? Otherwise answer yes.
      `the uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee, in dollars (or your best figure for it).
      `the legal costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs, in dollars, excluding disbursements and the uplift fee, otherwise payable.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 308 -- is my uplift fee allowed` `the agreement is conditional` `the uplift basis is separately identified` `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` `the uplift fee` `the legal costs otherwise payable` MEANS
    `s 308 -- the uplift fee is allowed` `the agreement is conditional` `the uplift basis is separately identified` `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` `the uplift fee` `the legal costs otherwise payable`

-- s 309(1): "A law practice must not enter into a costs agreement under which the amount payable to the law practice,
-- or any part of that amount, is calculated by reference to the amount of any award or settlement or the value of any
-- property that may be recovered in any proceedings to which the agreement relates. Penalty: Fine not exceeding 100
-- penalty units." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply to the extent that the costs agreement adopts an applicable scale
-- of costs."
@export Is my costs agreement a prohibited contingency fee -- does it charge a share of what I win (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 309)? A law practice must not make a costs agreement under which any of the amount payable to it is calculated by reference to the amount of an award or settlement, or the value of property recovered, in the proceedings (except so far as the agreement adopts an applicable scale of costs). It is an offence (up to 100 penalty units); the agreement is void and the law practice can recover nothing for its services in the matter (s 311(5)).
GIVEN `the fee is worked out from what I recover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is any part of the amount payable to the law practice calculated by reference to the amount of an award or settlement, or the value of property, that may be recovered in the proceedings (for example "30% of your payout")?
      `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is that so only because the agreement adopts an applicable scale of costs that works that way?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 309 -- is it a prohibited contingency fee` `the fee is worked out from what I recover` `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs` MEANS
    `the fee is worked out from what I recover` AND (NOT `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs`)

-- s 311(1): "A costs agreement that contravenes, or is entered into in contravention of, any provision of this Division
-- is void." The contraventions are reported most serious first, by their consequence (fork F8): s 309 (nothing
-- recoverable, s 311(5)), s 308 (no uplift fee, s 311(4)), then ss 307 and 306 (s 311(2), (3)). s 307 applies only to a
-- conditional agreement.
DECLARE `Is my agreement void` IS ONE OF
    `not void under s 311: no contravention of Division 5 on these facts`
    `void: it charges a share of what I recover (s 309), so the law practice can recover nothing for its services (s 311(5))`
    `void: its uplift fee breaks s 308, so the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 311(4))`
    `void: it breaks the conditional costs agreement rules (s 307), so costs are recoverable only as s 303 allows, capped at the agreement (s 311(2), (3))`
    `void: it is not properly made (s 306), so costs are recoverable only as s 303 allows, capped at the agreement (s 311(2), (3))`

@export Is my costs agreement void, and what follows (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 306 to 309, 311)? A costs agreement that breaks any rule of Division 5 is void: one charging a share of what you recover (s 309) leaves the law practice unable to recover anything for its services; one with an unlawful uplift fee (s 308) loses the uplift fee; one breaking the no-win no-fee rules (s 307) or not properly made (s 306: in writing, a proper offer, no contracting out of assessment) leaves the costs recoverable only on the scale or at fair value, never more than the agreement would have given.
GIVEN `it is written or evidenced in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the costs agreement written, or evidenced in writing?
      `it was made by my accepting a written offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it made by your accepting (in writing or by conduct) a written offer from the law practice?
      `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, did the offer clearly state that it was an offer to enter a costs agreement, that it could be accepted in writing or by other conduct, and what conduct would be acceptance? Otherwise answer yes.
      `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the agreement say that the legal costs are not subject to costs assessment?
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a sophisticated client (see the s 283 question)? An individual usually is not.
      `the agreement is conditional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is payment of some or all of the costs conditional on a successful outcome (no-win no-fee)?
      `the kind of matter` IS A `The kind of matter` @desc What kind of matter is it?
      `it sets out what counts as success` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If conditional: does it set out the circumstances that make up a successful outcome? Otherwise answer yes.
      `it is in clear plain language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it in clear plain language?
      `I signed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you sign it?
      `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If conditional: does it state that you were informed of your right to seek independent legal advice first? Otherwise answer yes.
      `the cooling-off business days it gives` IS A NUMBER @desc If conditional: how many clear business days of cooling-off does it give? Otherwise 5.
      `it has an uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it provide for an uplift fee (additional legal costs, excluding disbursements, payable on a successful outcome)?
      `the uplift basis is separately identified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, is the basis of calculating it separately identified? Otherwise answer yes.
      `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, does it contain an estimate (or a range and the major variables)? Otherwise answer yes.
      `the matter is litigious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the matter involve, or is it likely to involve, court or tribunal proceedings?
      `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If there is an uplift fee in a litigious matter: did the law practice reasonably believe success was reasonably likely? Otherwise answer yes.
      `the uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee in dollars, or 0.
      `the legal costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs in dollars, excluding disbursements and the uplift fee.
      `the fee is worked out from what I recover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is any part of the amount payable calculated by reference to the amount of an award or settlement, or the value of property recovered?
      `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, is that only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs? Otherwise answer no.
GIVETH A `Is my agreement void`
`s 311 -- is my costs agreement void` `it is written or evidenced in writing` `it was made by my accepting a written offer` `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires` `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment` `I am a sophisticated client` `the agreement is conditional` `the kind of matter` `it sets out what counts as success` `it is in clear plain language` `I signed it` `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `it has an uplift fee` `the uplift basis is separately identified` `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` `the uplift fee` `the legal costs otherwise payable` `the fee is worked out from what I recover` `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 309 -- is it a prohibited contingency fee` `the fee is worked out from what I recover` `that is only because it adopts an applicable scale of costs`
            THEN `void: it charges a share of what I recover (s 309), so the law practice can recover nothing for its services (s 311(5))`
        IF `it has an uplift fee` AND (NOT (`s 308 -- the uplift fee is allowed` `the agreement is conditional` `the uplift basis is separately identified` `it contains an estimate of the uplift fee` `the matter is litigious` `the law practice reasonably believed success was reasonably likely` `the uplift fee` `the legal costs otherwise payable`))
            THEN `void: its uplift fee breaks s 308, so the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 311(4))`
        IF `the agreement is conditional` AND (NOT (`s 307 -- does my no-win no-fee agreement meet the requirements` `the kind of matter` `it sets out what counts as success` `it is written or evidenced in writing` `it is in clear plain language` `I signed it` `it states I was told of my right to independent legal advice` `the cooling-off business days it gives` `I am a sophisticated client`))
            THEN `void: it breaks the conditional costs agreement rules (s 307), so costs are recoverable only as s 303 allows, capped at the agreement (s 311(2), (3))`
        IF (NOT (`s 306 -- the agreement is properly made` `it is written or evidenced in writing` `it was made by my accepting a written offer` `the offer stated the three things s 306(4) requires` `it says my costs are not subject to costs assessment` `I am a sophisticated client`))
            THEN `void: it is not properly made (s 306), so costs are recoverable only as s 303 allows, capped at the agreement (s 311(2), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `not void under s 311: no contravention of Division 5 on these facts`

-- s 311(3): "a law practice is not entitled to recover any amount in excess of the amount that the law practice would
-- have been entitled to recover if the costs agreement had not been void and must repay any excess amount received."
-- (4): for s 308, it "is not entitled to recover the whole or any part of the uplift fee and must repay the amount
-- received in respect of the uplift fee". (5): for s 309, it "is not entitled to recover any amount in respect of the
-- provision of legal services in the matter ... and must repay any amount received in respect of those services". (6):
-- an unpaid repayment is recoverable as a debt. What the costs come to under s 303 is the person's figure (an
-- assessment); fork F9 for how (3) and (4) combine.
DECLARE `What was wrong with the agreement` IS ONE OF
    `it charged a share of what I recovered (s 309)`
    `its uplift fee broke s 308`
    `it broke another rule of Division 5 (s 306 or s 307)`
    `nothing: it is not void`

@export How much must the law practice repay me because my costs agreement was void (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 311(3) to (6))? If the agreement charged a share of what you recovered (s 309): everything you paid for its services. If its uplift fee broke s 308: the uplift fee you paid, and anything else you paid above what the scale or a fair value allows (capped at the agreement without the uplift). Otherwise: whatever you paid above the lesser of the scale or fair value and what the agreement would have given. If it does not repay, you can sue for the amount as a debt.
GIVEN `what was wrong with the agreement` IS A `What was wrong with the agreement` @desc What made the agreement void (see the s 311 question)?
      `what I paid apart from any uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc How much you have paid the law practice for its services in the matter, in dollars, apart from any uplift fee.
      `the uplift fee I paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of an uplift fee you have paid, in dollars, or 0.
      `the costs on the scale or at fair value` IS A NUMBER @desc What the legal costs come to on the applicable scale, or at the fair and reasonable value of the services (s 303), in dollars, as assessed or agreed.
      `what the agreement would have given apart from uplift` IS A NUMBER @desc What the law practice would have been entitled to under the agreement had it not been void, in dollars, apart from any uplift fee.
      `the uplift fee under the agreement` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee the agreement provided for, in dollars, or 0.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 311 -- how much must the law practice repay me` `what was wrong with the agreement` `what I paid apart from any uplift fee` `the uplift fee I paid` `the costs on the scale or at fair value` `what the agreement would have given apart from uplift` `the uplift fee under the agreement` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was wrong with the agreement`
    WHEN `it charged a share of what I recovered (s 309)` THEN `what I paid apart from any uplift fee` PLUS `the uplift fee I paid`
    WHEN `its uplift fee broke s 308` THEN `the uplift fee I paid` PLUS (`the larger of` 0 (`what I paid apart from any uplift fee` MINUS (`the smaller of` `the costs on the scale or at fair value` `what the agreement would have given apart from uplift`)))
    WHEN `it broke another rule of Division 5 (s 306 or s 307)` THEN `the larger of` 0 (`what I paid in all` MINUS (`the smaller of` `the costs on the scale or at fair value` `the agreement in all`))
    WHEN `nothing: it is not void` THEN 0
    WHERE
        `what I paid in all` MEANS `what I paid apart from any uplift fee` PLUS `the uplift fee I paid`
        `the agreement in all` MEANS `what the agreement would have given apart from uplift` PLUS `the uplift fee under the agreement`

-- s 312(1): "On application by a client, the costs assessor may order that a costs agreement be set aside if
-- satisfied that the agreement is not fair or reasonable." (12): "client means a person to whom or for whom legal
-- services are or have been provided." s 306(6): for an agreement between the law practice and an associated third
-- party payer to which the client is not a party, "client" in s 312 is the payer. s 300(3): "If a law practice does
-- not disclose to a client or an associated third party payer anything required by this Division to be disclosed and
-- the client or associated third party payer has entered into a costs agreement with the law practice, the client or
-- associated third party payer may also apply under section 312 for the costs agreement to be set aside." So an
-- associated third party payer who is a party alongside the client may apply too, if not given a disclosure it was
-- owed. Whether the agreement is set aside is the costs assessor's judgment: the answer says so.
DECLARE `Who I am for setting aside` IS ONE OF
    `the client (the person the legal services are for)`
    `an associated third party payer who made the agreement, without the client being a party`
    `an associated third party payer who made the agreement together with the client`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I seek to set it aside` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I may apply, and the costs assessor may set it aside if satisfied it is not fair or reasonable (s 312(1))`
    `yes: I may apply because the law practice did not make a disclosure owed to me (s 300(3)), and the costs assessor may set it aside if satisfied it is not fair or reasonable (s 312(1))`
    `no: only the client, an associated third party payer party to the agreement without the client, or one not given a disclosure it was owed may apply (ss 300(3), 312(1), (12), 306(6))`

@export Can I apply to have my costs agreement set aside as unfair or unreasonable (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 300(3), 312(1))? The client may apply to a costs assessor, who may set the agreement aside if satisfied it is not fair or reasonable (looking at, among other things, fraud or misrepresentation, the practitioner's conduct, any failure to disclose costs, and the circumstances when and after it was made). An associated third party payer who made the agreement without the client may apply too, and one who made it together with the client may apply if the law practice did not disclose to them something Division 3 required.
GIVEN `who I am` IS A `Who I am for setting aside` @desc Who are you, in relation to the costs agreement?
      `the law practice did not make a disclosure owed to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the law practice fail to disclose to you something Division 3 (costs disclosure) required it to disclose to you?
GIVETH A `Can I seek to set it aside`
`s 312 -- can I apply to set aside my costs agreement` `who I am` `the law practice did not make a disclosure owed to me` MEANS
    IF `who I am` EQUALS `the client (the person the legal services are for)`
       OR `who I am` EQUALS `an associated third party payer who made the agreement, without the client being a party`
    THEN `yes: I may apply, and the costs assessor may set it aside if satisfied it is not fair or reasonable (s 312(1))`
    ELSE IF `who I am` EQUALS `an associated third party payer who made the agreement together with the client`
            AND `the law practice did not make a disclosure owed to me`
         THEN `yes: I may apply because the law practice did not make a disclosure owed to me (s 300(3)), and the costs assessor may set it aside if satisfied it is not fair or reasonable (s 312(1))`
         ELSE `no: only the client, an associated third party payer party to the agreement without the client, or one not given a disclosure it was owed may apply (ss 300(3), 312(1), (12), 306(6))`

-- s 312(5): the assessor "must apply the applicable scale of costs (if any); or (b) if there is no applicable scale of
-- costs, ... must determine the fair and reasonable legal costs". (6): "the costs assessor may not order the payment of
-- an amount in excess of the amount that the law practice would have been entitled to recover if the costs agreement
-- had not been set aside."
@export If my costs agreement is set aside, what is the most I can be ordered to pay (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 312(5), (6))? The costs assessor applies the applicable scale of costs, or if none determines the fair and reasonable costs, and cannot order more than the law practice would have been entitled to under the agreement.
GIVEN `the costs on the scale or at fair value` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs, in dollars, on the applicable scale or, if none, as the fair and reasonable costs the assessor determines.
      `what the agreement would have given` IS A NUMBER @desc What the law practice would have been entitled to recover under the agreement had it not been set aside, in dollars.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 312(6) -- the most I can be ordered to pay` `the costs on the scale or at fair value` `what the agreement would have given` MEANS
    `the smaller of` `the costs on the scale or at fair value` `what the agreement would have given`

§§ `Division 6 -- bills, itemised bills, and when I can be sued`

-- s 316(1): "If a bill is given by a law practice in the form of a lump sum bill, any person who is entitled to apply
-- for an assessment of the legal costs to which the bill relates may request the law practice to give the person an
-- itemised bill." Fork F10: "entitled to apply" is read as being a client or third party payer who may apply under
-- s 319, without testing the 60 days. "If": otherwise a plain no.
@export Can I ask the law practice for an itemised bill (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 316(1))? Yes, if it gave you a lump sum bill (one that describes the services and states only the total) and you are a person entitled to apply for a costs assessment of it: the client, or a third party payer for the costs you must pay. It must give the itemised bill within 21 days.
GIVEN `the bill is a lump sum bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the bill a lump sum bill (describing the services and stating the total), rather than an itemised bill?
      `I am the client or a third party payer for these costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the client, or a person liable to pay these costs as a third party payer?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 316(1) -- can I ask for an itemised bill` `the bill is a lump sum bill` `I am the client or a third party payer for these costs` MEANS
    `the bill is a lump sum bill` AND `I am the client or a third party payer for these costs`

-- s 316(2): "The law practice must comply with the request within 21 days after the date on which the request is
-- made."
@export By what day must the law practice give me the itemised bill I asked for (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 316(2))? Within 21 days after the day you made the request.
GIVEN `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked the law practice for an itemised bill. ISO date.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 316(2) -- by what day must I get the itemised bill` `the day I asked` MEANS
    `days after` `the day I asked` 21

DECLARE `Itemised bill party` IS ONE OF `the law practice`
DECLARE `Itemised bill action` IS ONE OF `give me the itemised bill I asked for (s 316(2))`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Itemised bill party` `Itemised bill action`
`s 316(2) -- the law practice must give me the itemised bill` MEANS
    PARTY `the law practice`
    MUST `give me the itemised bill I asked for (s 316(2))`
    WITHIN 21
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the law practice` BECAUSE "s 316(2): no itemised bill within 21 days after the request"

DECLARE `The itemised bill's timing` IS ONE OF
    `on time, or can still be (s 316(2))`
    `late: no itemised bill within 21 days after my request (s 316(2))`

-- A bill not yet given stands for "had it been given on the day asked about".
@export Is the law practice late in giving me the itemised bill I asked for (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 316(2))? It must give it within 21 days after your request. Until it does, it cannot sue you for the costs (s 316(5)).
GIVEN `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for an itemised bill. ISO date.
      `I have received it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you received the itemised bill?
      `the day I received it` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was given. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (usually today). ISO date.
GIVETH A `The itemised bill's timing`
`s 316(2) -- is the law practice late with my itemised bill` `the day I asked` `I have received it` `the day I received it` `the day asked about` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 316(2) -- the law practice must give me the itemised bill` 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `on time, or can still be (s 316(2))`
    OTHERWISE `late: no itemised bill within 21 days after my request (s 316(2))`
    WHERE
        `the day given` MEANS IF `I have received it` THEN `the day I received it` ELSE `the day asked about`
        `the events` MEANS LIST (EVENT `the law practice` `give me the itemised bill I asked for (s 316(2))` (`the days from` `the day I asked` `to` `the day given`))

-- s 313(1): "A law practice must not commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from a person until at least 30
-- days after the law practice has given a bill to the person in accordance with sections 314 and 315." s 316(4): "a law
-- practice must not commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from a person who has been given a lump sum bill
-- until at least 30 days after the date on which the person is given the bill." (5): "If the person makes a request
-- for an itemised bill ..., the law practice must not commence legal proceedings ... until at least 30 days after
-- complying with the request." Allowed on the 30th day after: the SHANT runs for 29 days.
`the days before I can be sued` MEANS 30

DECLARE `Suing party` IS ONE OF `the law practice`
DECLARE `Suing action` IS ONE OF `commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from me`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Suing party` `Suing action`
`ss 313(1), 316(4), (5) -- the law practice must not sue me for 30 days` MEANS
    PARTY `the law practice`
    SHANT `commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from me`
    WITHIN (`the days before I can be sued` MINUS 1)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the law practice` BECAUSE "ss 313(1), 316(4), (5): proceedings for costs commenced less than 30 days after the bill (or the itemised bill); the court must stay them (s 313(3))"

-- When the 30 days run from: the bill; or, where I asked for an itemised bill after a lump sum bill, the day it was
-- given. NOTHING while an itemised bill I asked for has not yet been given (no day can yet be named).
@export From what day can the law practice sue me for its legal costs (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 313(1), 316(4), (5))? Not until 30 days after it gave you a bill (one signed and with the statement of your rights); if you asked for an itemised bill after a lump sum bill, not until 30 days after it gives you the itemised bill. While the itemised bill has not been given, no day can be named (nothing). Other bars (a pending costs assessment, a failure to disclose) are in the next question.
GIVEN `the day the bill was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you its bill (with the written statement of your rights, unless you are a sophisticated client). ISO date.
      `I asked for an itemised bill after a lump sum bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the bill a lump sum bill, and did you ask for an itemised bill?
      `the itemised bill has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, has the law practice given you the itemised bill? Otherwise answer no.
      `the day the itemised bill was given` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was given. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`ss 313, 316 -- from what day can I be sued for costs` `the day the bill was given` `I asked for an itemised bill after a lump sum bill` `the itemised bill has been given` `the day the itemised bill was given` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I asked for an itemised bill after a lump sum bill`)
    THEN JUST (`days after` `the day the bill was given` `the days before I can be sued`)
    ELSE IF `the itemised bill has been given`
         THEN JUST (`whichever is later of` (`days after` `the day the bill was given` `the days before I can be sued`) (`days after` `the day the itemised bill was given` `the days before I can be sued`))
         ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Can I be sued for costs` IS ONE OF
    `Part 3.3 does not stop the law practice suing me on that day`
    `no: no bill has been given to me (s 313(1))`
    `no: my costs assessment application has not been completed (s 323(b))`
    `no: I was not told something Division 3 required, and the costs have not been assessed (s 300(2))`
    `no: I asked for an itemised bill and it has not been given (s 316(5))`
    `no: it is less than 30 days after the bill or itemised bill, and the court must stay the proceedings (ss 313(1), (3), 316(4), (5))`

-- s 300(2): a law practice that did not disclose "may not maintain proceedings against the client ... for the recovery
-- of legal costs unless the costs have been assessed under Division 7." s 323(b): if an application for a costs
-- assessment is made, "the law practice must not commence any proceedings to recover the legal costs until the costs
-- assessment has been completed." s 313(2): a court may authorise proceedings sooner if a bill was given and the person
-- is about to leave Tasmania. s 313(3): proceedings in contravention of (1) must be stayed. Fork F11: a s 313(2) order
-- lifts only the s 313(1) bar; for a lump sum bill s 316(4), (5) still bar proceedings for 30 days (finding 3).
@export Can the law practice start court proceedings against me to recover its legal costs on a given day (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 300(2), 313, 316, 323)? Not before it gives you a bill; not while a costs assessment you applied for is under way; not, if it failed to tell you something it had to about costs, until the costs have been assessed; not while an itemised bill you asked for is outstanding; and not until 30 days after the bill (or the itemised bill). A court may let it sue sooner on an itemised bill if you are about to leave Tasmania.
GIVEN `a bill has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the law practice given you a bill (signed, and with the written statement of your rights unless you are a sophisticated client)?
      `the day the bill was given` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was given. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the bill is a lump sum bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a lump sum bill (rather than an itemised bill)?
      `I asked for an itemised bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is a lump sum bill, did you ask for an itemised bill? Otherwise answer no.
      `the itemised bill has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you asked, has the itemised bill been given? Otherwise answer no.
      `the day the itemised bill was given` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `my costs assessment is under way` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you (or someone else) applied for a costs assessment of these costs, and is it not yet completed?
      `something required was not disclosed to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the law practice fail to disclose to you something Division 3 required (and not solely because of a law practice it retained)?
      `the costs have been assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the costs been assessed under Division 7?
      `a court allowed it to sue sooner because I am about to leave Tasmania` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a court made an order under s 313(2) authorising the law practice to sue you sooner, because you are about to leave Tasmania?
      `the day it sues` IS A DATE @desc The day the proceedings are, or would be, commenced. ISO date.
GIVETH A `Can I be sued for costs`
`ss 300, 313, 316, 323 -- can the law practice sue me for costs` `a bill has been given` `the day the bill was given` `the bill is a lump sum bill` `I asked for an itemised bill` `the itemised bill has been given` `the day the itemised bill was given` `my costs assessment is under way` `something required was not disclosed to me` `the costs have been assessed` `a court allowed it to sue sooner because I am about to leave Tasmania` `the day it sues` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `a bill has been given`)
            THEN `no: no bill has been given to me (s 313(1))`
        IF `my costs assessment is under way`
            THEN `no: my costs assessment application has not been completed (s 323(b))`
        IF `something required was not disclosed to me` AND (NOT `the costs have been assessed`)
            THEN `no: I was not told something Division 3 required, and the costs have not been assessed (s 300(2))`
        IF `the bill is a lump sum bill` AND `I asked for an itemised bill` AND (NOT `the itemised bill has been given`)
            THEN `no: I asked for an itemised bill and it has not been given (s 316(5))`
        IF `too early`
            THEN `no: it is less than 30 days after the bill or itemised bill, and the court must stay the proceedings (ss 313(1), (3), 316(4), (5))`
        OTHERWISE `Part 3.3 does not stop the law practice suing me on that day`
    WHERE
        `it keeps away for 30 days from` `the start` MEANS
            CONSIDER EVALTRACE `ss 313(1), 316(4), (5) -- the law practice must not sue me for 30 days` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the law practice` `commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from me` (`the days from` `the start` `to` `the day it sues`)))
            WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `the s 313 bar holds` MEANS
            (NOT `a court allowed it to sue sooner because I am about to leave Tasmania`)
            AND (NOT (`it keeps away for 30 days from` `the day the bill was given`))
        `the s 316(4) bar holds` MEANS
            `the bill is a lump sum bill` AND (NOT (`it keeps away for 30 days from` `the day the bill was given`))
        `the s 316(5) bar holds` MEANS
                `the bill is a lump sum bill`
            AND `I asked for an itemised bill`
            AND (NOT (`it keeps away for 30 days from` `the day the itemised bill was given`))
        `too early` MEANS `the s 313 bar holds` OR `the s 316(4) bar holds` OR `the s 316(5) bar holds`

§§ `Division 7 -- costs assessment`

-- s 319(5): "An application by a client or third party payer for a costs assessment under this section must be made
-- within 60 days after -- (a) the bill was given or the request for payment was made to the client or third party
-- payer; or (b) the costs were paid if neither a bill was given nor a request was made."
`the days to apply for assessment` MEANS 60

@export By what day must I apply for an assessment of my legal costs (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 319(5))? Within 60 days after the bill was given or payment was requested; or, if there was neither, within 60 days after you paid. You may apply even if you have already paid.
GIVEN `I was given a bill or asked to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a bill, or asked for payment?
      `the day of the bill or request` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the bill was given or payment requested. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I paid` IS A DATE @desc If there was neither, the day you paid the costs. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 319(5) -- by what day must I apply for a costs assessment` `I was given a bill or asked to pay` `the day of the bill or request` `the day I paid` MEANS
    IF `I was given a bill or asked to pay`
    THEN `days after` `the day of the bill or request` `the days to apply for assessment`
    ELSE `days after` `the day I paid` `the days to apply for assessment`

DECLARE `Can my assessment go ahead` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the application is in time (s 319(5))`
    `out of time, but it may be dealt with if the Supreme Court decides that is just and fair, having regard to the delay and its reasons (s 319(6))`
    `no: out of time, and a sophisticated client cannot have the time extended (s 319(6))`
    `no: as a sophisticated client I contracted out of costs assessment (s 338)`

-- s 319(1), (2): a client, or a third party payer for costs it must pay, may apply. (6): an application out of time,
-- "otherwise than by (a) a sophisticated client; or (b) a third party payer who would be a sophisticated client", "may
-- be dealt with ... if the Supreme Court ... determines ... that it is just and fair". s 338: a sophisticated client
-- (or such a payer) "may contract out of this Division". The Supreme Court's decision is answered conditionally.
@export Can I still have my legal costs assessed by a costs assessor (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 319, 338)? A client, or a third party payer for the costs it must pay, may apply within 60 days after the bill or request for payment (or after paying, if there was neither). A later application may still be dealt with if the Supreme Court decides that is just and fair, unless you are a sophisticated client. A sophisticated client who contracted out of costs assessment cannot apply.
GIVEN `I was given a bill or asked to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a bill, or asked for payment?
      `the day of the bill or request` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I paid` IS A DATE @desc If there was neither, the day you paid. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or would apply) for the costs assessment. ISO date.
      `I am a sophisticated client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a sophisticated client (or a third party payer who would be one if you were the client)? An individual usually is not.
      `I contracted out of costs assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a sophisticated client, did you contract out of costs assessment? Otherwise answer no.
GIVETH A `Can my assessment go ahead`
`s 319 -- can my costs be assessed` `I was given a bill or asked to pay` `the day of the bill or request` `the day I paid` `the day I apply` `I am a sophisticated client` `I contracted out of costs assessment` MEANS
    IF `I am a sophisticated client` AND `I contracted out of costs assessment`
    THEN `no: as a sophisticated client I contracted out of costs assessment (s 338)`
    ELSE IF `the day I apply` `is on or before` (`s 319(5) -- by what day must I apply for a costs assessment` `I was given a bill or asked to pay` `the day of the bill or request` `the day I paid`)
         THEN `yes: the application is in time (s 319(5))`
         ELSE IF `I am a sophisticated client`
              THEN `no: out of time, and a sophisticated client cannot have the time extended (s 319(6))`
              ELSE `out of time, but it may be dealt with if the Supreme Court decides that is just and fair, having regard to the delay and its reasons (s 319(6))`

-- s 321(4): "An application may not be made under this section unless at least 30 days have passed since -- (a) the
-- bill was given or the request for payment was made; or (b) the costs were paid, if neither a bill was given nor a
-- request was made; or (c) an application has been made under this Division by another person in respect of the legal
-- costs." Fork F12: the alternatives are read disjunctively, so the earliest day any of them allows.
@export From what day can the law practice itself apply to have its bill to me assessed (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 321(4))? Not until 30 days have passed since the bill or request for payment (or since you paid, if there was neither), or since someone else applied for assessment of the costs, whichever comes first.
GIVEN `I was given a bill or asked to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were you given a bill, or asked for payment?
      `the day of the bill or request` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I paid` IS A DATE @desc If there was neither, the day you paid. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `someone else has applied for assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you or someone else applied for an assessment of these costs?
      `the day of that application` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day of the application. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 321(4) -- from what day can the law practice apply for assessment` `I was given a bill or asked to pay` `the day of the bill or request` `the day I paid` `someone else has applied for assessment` `the day of that application` MEANS
    IF `someone else has applied for assessment` AND `the day of that application` `is on or before` `the day of the bill or payment`
    THEN `days after` `the day of that application` 30
    ELSE `days after` `the day of the bill or payment` 30
    WHERE
        `the day of the bill or payment` MEANS IF `I was given a bill or asked to pay` THEN `the day of the bill or request` ELSE `the day I paid`

-- s 328(1): "A costs assessor must assess the amount of any disputed costs that are subject to a costs agreement by
-- reference to the provisions of the costs agreement if -- (a) a relevant provision of the costs agreement specifies
-- the amount, or a rate or other means for calculating the amount, of the costs; and (b) the agreement has not been set
-- aside under section 312 -- unless the assessor is satisfied -- (c) that the agreement does not comply in a material
-- respect with any applicable disclosure requirements of Division 3; or (d) that Division 5 precludes the law practice
-- concerned from recovering the amount of the costs; or (e) that the parties otherwise agree." Outside (a) and (b),
-- s 328 does not require it, and the assessor applies s 327 (fair and reasonable): a plain no to this question.
@export Will the costs assessor assess my disputed costs by the terms of my costs agreement (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 328)? Yes, if the agreement specifies the amount (or a rate or way of calculating it) and has not been set aside, unless the assessor is satisfied that the agreement does not comply in a material respect with the disclosure rules, that Division 5 stops the law practice recovering the amount, or that you and the law practice agree otherwise. If not, the assessor decides what is fair and reasonable (s 327).
GIVEN `the agreement specifies the amount, rate or method` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a provision of your costs agreement specify the amount of the disputed costs, or a rate or other way of calculating it?
      `the agreement has been set aside` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the agreement been set aside under s 312?
      `the assessor is satisfied the disclosure rules were materially broken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the costs assessor satisfied that the agreement does not comply in a material respect with the disclosure requirements of Division 3?
      `the assessor is satisfied Division 5 precludes recovery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the costs assessor satisfied that Division 5 (for example a void agreement) stops the law practice recovering the amount?
      `we agreed otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the law practice agreed that the costs should not be assessed by the agreement?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 328 -- will my costs be assessed by my costs agreement` `the agreement specifies the amount, rate or method` `the agreement has been set aside` `the assessor is satisfied the disclosure rules were materially broken` `the assessor is satisfied Division 5 precludes recovery` `we agreed otherwise` MEANS
        `the agreement specifies the amount, rate or method`
    AND (NOT `the agreement has been set aside`)
    AND (NOT (   `the assessor is satisfied the disclosure rules were materially broken`
              OR `the assessor is satisfied Division 5 precludes recovery`
              OR `we agreed otherwise`))

-- s 331(2): "Unless the costs assessor otherwise orders and subject to subsection (5), the law practice to which the
-- legal costs are payable or were paid must pay the costs of the assessment if -- (a) on the assessment the legal costs
-- are reduced by 15% or more; or (b) the costs assessor is satisfied that the law practice failed to comply with
-- Division 3." (3): otherwise "the costs of the assessment must be paid by the party ordered by the costs assessor".
`the reduction that shifts the costs of assessment` MEANS 15%

DECLARE `Who pays for the assessment` IS ONE OF
    `the law practice pays the costs of the assessment, unless the assessor orders otherwise (s 331(2))`
    `whoever the costs assessor orders to pay them (s 331(2), (3))`

@export Who pays the costs of my costs assessment (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 331(2), (3))? The law practice, unless the costs assessor orders otherwise, if the assessment cuts the legal costs by 15% or more, or the assessor is satisfied the law practice failed to make the required costs disclosure. Otherwise, whoever the assessor orders to pay.
GIVEN `the costs billed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs claimed (billed) that were assessed, in dollars.
      `the costs as assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs as allowed on the assessment, in dollars.
      `the assessor is satisfied the law practice failed to disclose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the costs assessor satisfied that the law practice failed to comply with the disclosure rules of Division 3?
      `the assessor ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the costs assessor ordered otherwise (or the Supreme Court made another order on a referral)?
GIVETH A `Who pays for the assessment`
`s 331 -- who pays for my costs assessment` `the costs billed` `the costs as assessed` `the assessor is satisfied the law practice failed to disclose` `the assessor ordered otherwise` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the assessor ordered otherwise`)
       AND (   (`the costs billed` MINUS `the costs as assessed`) AT LEAST (`the costs billed` TIMES `the reduction that shifts the costs of assessment`)
            OR `the assessor is satisfied the law practice failed to disclose`)
    THEN `the law practice pays the costs of the assessment, unless the assessor orders otherwise (s 331(2))`
    ELSE `whoever the costs assessor orders to pay them (s 331(2), (3))`

-- s 331(6): "The costs assessor may order that the party who is liable to pay the costs of assessment pay to the Court
-- a fee of not more than 4% of the amount allowed on the costs assessment."
`the most assessment fee` MEANS 4%

@export If I must pay the costs of the assessment, what is the most the Court's fee can be (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 331(6))? The costs assessor may order the party liable for the costs of the assessment to pay the Court a fee of up to 4% of the amount allowed on the assessment.
GIVEN `the amount allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount allowed on the costs assessment, in dollars.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 331(6) -- the largest assessment fee` `the amount allowed` MEANS
    `the amount allowed` TIMES `the most assessment fee`

-- s 333(1): "A party who is dissatisfied with the outcome of an assessment may object before the final certificate is
-- signed." s 334(1): "A party who objects under section 333 and who is dissatisfied with the certificate ... may apply
-- to the Supreme Court to review the assessment as to that item". (2): "within 14 days after the date of the
-- certificate, or any other time that the costs assessor at the time of the signing of the certificate may allow,
-- whichever is the later."
@export By what day must I apply to the Supreme Court to review my costs assessment (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) s 334(2))? Within 14 days after the date of the costs assessor's certificate, or by any later day the assessor allowed when signing it, whichever is later.
GIVEN `the day of the certificate` IS A DATE @desc The date of the costs assessor's certificate. ISO date.
      `the assessor allowed more time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When signing the certificate, did the costs assessor allow a different time to apply?
      `the last day the assessor allowed` IS A DATE @desc If so, the last day of that time. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
GIVETH A DATE
`s 334(2) -- by what day must I apply for review` `the day of the certificate` `the assessor allowed more time` `the last day the assessor allowed` MEANS
    IF `the assessor allowed more time`
    THEN `whichever is later of` (`days after` `the day of the certificate` 14) `the last day the assessor allowed`
    ELSE `days after` `the day of the certificate` 14

-- "A party who objects under section 333": an objection made before the final certificate was signed is a condition;
-- the period is one the text fixes. Outside either, a plain no.
@export Can I ask the Supreme Court to review my costs assessment (Legal Profession Act 2007 (Tas) ss 333, 334)? Only if you objected to the costs assessor, in writing with your grounds, before the final certificate was signed, and you apply within 14 days after the certificate (or a later time the assessor allowed). The review is of the items you objected to; the rest of the certificate is final.
GIVEN `I objected before the final certificate was signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you object to the assessment under s 333, in writing with the items and grounds, before the final certificate was signed?
      `the day of the certificate` IS A DATE @desc The date of the certificate. ISO date.
      `the assessor allowed more time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the costs assessor, when signing, allow a different time to apply?
      `the last day the assessor allowed` IS A DATE @desc If so, the last day of that time. Otherwise any date; it is ignored.
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or would apply) to the Supreme Court. ISO date.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 334 -- can I ask the Supreme Court to review` `I objected before the final certificate was signed` `the day of the certificate` `the assessor allowed more time` `the last day the assessor allowed` `the day I apply` MEANS
        `I objected before the final certificate was signed`
    AND `the day I apply` `is on or before` (`s 334(2) -- by what day must I apply for review` `the day of the certificate` `the assessor allowed more time` `the last day the assessor allowed`)
